      meaning:
        "He who is known as the son of the sage Subrahmaṇya (Trivikrama Paṇḍita), an intense devotee of the glorious lotus feet of Keśava and of Ānandatīrtha, composed this stuti of Hari and of this Vāyu-deva. Those who, out of reverence for the worship of their feet, constantly bow to those two adorable ones with this shining garland woven of words, become endowed with great virtues and go to Mukti. — The son of Subrahmaṇya Paṇḍita, Śrī Trivikrama Paṇḍita, addressed this lyrical poem to Śrī Hari in His forms of Rāma, Kṛṣṇa and Veda Vyāsa, and to Śrī Vāyu in his three avatāras — Hanumanta, Bhīmasena and Ānandatīrtha. Those who read it daily, praising both Hari and Vāyu, are freed from mundane attachments and quickly take the path towards Mokṣa.",
